Material adapted, by letibot file:

  crates/ui/src/editor.rs
      Adapted from packages/tui/src/component/prompt/index.tsx and
      packages/tui/src/prompt/history.tsx —
        * the two-counter interrupt model: Esc twice within 5 s interrupts,
          Ctrl+C twice within 1 s quits and only from an empty composer, with
          the hint text changing after the first press (index.tsx:424-455,
          887-906);
        * a large paste collapsing to a "[Pasted ~N lines]" placeholder at
          >= 5 lines or > 800 characters, expanded on submit (index.tsx:1307-1355;
          the 800-character threshold is a Kilo Code change, marked #7252);
        * CRLF and bare-CR normalisation on paste, for Windows ConPTY
          (index.tsx:1555-1579);
        * the composer never exceeding a third of the screen, floor 6 rows
          (index.tsx:1494);
        * history as a capped ring of 50 with consecutive duplicates dropped,
          and navigation refusing to move once a recalled entry has been edited
          (history.tsx).
      CHANGED: opencode tracks paste placeholders with extmarks — ranges that
      survive arbitrary editing. letibot keys the ledger on the placeholder text
      itself, so a deleted placeholder drops its paste and an edited one is
      submitted literally. This is a behavioural difference, chosen because it
      fails visibly rather than needing a range-tracking structure.

  crates/ui/src/width.rs
      Design read: packages/tui/src/prompt/display.ts — display-column to
      byte-offset conversion over grapheme clusters. letibot's `locate` and
      `offset_at` do the same job; no code was copied (theirs is Intl.Segmenter
      plus Bun.stringWidth, neither of which exists in Rust).

  crates/tools/src/edit.rs, crates/tools/src/builtins/edit.rs
      Design read, no code taken: packages/opencode/src/tool/edit.ts (the nine
      replacers and their 0.65 Levenshtein similarity floor) and
      packages/core/src/tool/edit.ts (the exact-only rewrite, its deferral TODO
      at line 84, and the `writeIfUnchanged` compare-and-swap). letibot's
      relaxation ladder covers the same ground — trailing whitespace,
      indentation, inner whitespace, case, first/last-line anchors — and
      **inverts the decision**: a relaxed match is reported to the model with
      the exact bytes that are there, never applied. Their error strings are
      quoted in the module headers as the thing being improved on, not as text
      to reuse. The allow/ask/deny permission ladder in
      crates/tools/src/adjudicate.rs is opencode's permission *model*, which
      `docs/implementation-plan.md` §11.9 identifies as the part worth
      vendoring; the async ask path is not theirs — `permission.ask` is declared
      at plugin/index.ts:261 with zero trigger sites repo-wide.

Note for anyone repeating this survey: opencode's terminal *rendering* is not in
opencode. Markdown parsing, wrapping, unicode width, syntax highlighting, diff
layout and cell diffing all live in the third-party @opentui/core package, which
ships as a native binary. What is opencode's own, and what is credited above, is
policy: thresholds, key semantics, and truncation budgets.

